                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  27EF'                         EXADDR:
  27EF'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27F0'   32 2E3E'              	STA	SRNGF
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  27F3'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27F4'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  27F6'   CA 281F'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  27F9'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  27FA'   CD 2834'              	CALL	EXNUM
  27FD'   DA 281D'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2800'   22 2E3F'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2803'   22 2E41'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2806'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2807'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  2809'   C2 2816'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  280C'   13                    	INX	D
  280D'   CD 2834'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2810'   DA 281D'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2813'   22 2E41'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2816'                         EXA_YS:
  2816'   EB                    	XCHG
  2817'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2819'   32 2E3E'              	STA	SRNGF
  281C'   C9                    	RET
  281D'                         EXA_NO:
  281D'   EB                    	XCHG
  281E'   C9                    	RET
  281F'                         EXA_AL:
  281F'   23                    	INX	H
  2820'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2821'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2824'   22 2E3F'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2827'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  282A'   22 2E41'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  282D'   E1                    	POP	H
  282E'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2830'   32 2E3E'              	STA	SRNGF
  2833'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  2834'                         EXNUM:
  2834'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2835'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2837'   D8                    	RC
  2838'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  283A'   3F                    	CMC
  283B'   D8                    	RC
  283C'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  283F'                         EXN_LP:
  283F'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2840'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  2842'   DA 285C'              	JC	EXN_DN
  2845'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  2847'   D2 285C'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  284A'   13                    	INX	D
  284B'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  284C'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  284D'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  284F'   D5                    	PUSH	D
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  2850'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  2851'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  2852'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  2853'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  2854'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  2855'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  2856'   D1                    	POP	D
  2857'   09                    	DAD	B
  2858'   C1                    	POP	B
  2859'   C3 283F'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  285C'                         EXN_DN:
  285C'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  285D'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  285E'                         EX_S:
  285E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  285F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2860'   CA 28A2'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  2863'   32 2E43'              	STA	SDELIM
  2866'   23                    	INX	H
  2867'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  286A'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  286D'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  286E'   CD 28A8'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2871'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2872'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2873'   CA 28A2'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  2876'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  2879'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  287B'   32 2E0B'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  287E'   11 2E55'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2881'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2883'   CD 28A8'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2886'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2887'   32 2E54'              	STA	SREPLN
  288A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  288B'   32 2E44'              	STA	SGFLAG
  288E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  288F'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2891'   C2 289A'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2894'   23                    	INX	H
  2895'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2897'   32 2E44'              	STA	SGFLAG
  289A'                         EX_SG:
  289A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  289B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  289C'   C2 2629'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  289F'   C3 28C8'              	JMP	SUBGO
  28A2'                         SUBNOP:
  28A2'   21 26F1'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  28A5'   C3 2637'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-126


                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  28A8'                         SCOPY:
  28A8'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  28AA'                         SCP_LP:
  28AA'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  28AB'   B9                    	CMP	C
  28AC'   D2 28C5'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  28AF'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  28B0'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28B1'   CA 28C5'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  28B4'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28B5'   21 2E43'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  28B8'   BE                    	CMP	M
  28B9'   E1                    	POP	H
  28BA'   CA 28C4'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  28BD'   12                    	STAX	D
  28BE'   13                    	INX	D
  28BF'   23                    	INX	H
  28C0'   04                    	INR	B
  28C1'   C3 28AA'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  28C4'                         SCP_SK:
  28C4'   23                    	INX	H
  28C5'                         SCP_DN:
  28C5'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28C6'   12                    	STAX	D
  28C7'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  28C8'                         SUBGO:
  28C8'   CD 127A'              	CALL	GZONE
  28CB'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  28CE'   22 2E52'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  28D1'   21 2E4F'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  28D4'   CD 0429'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  28D7'   AF                    	XRA	A
  28D8'   32 2E45'              	STA	SANY
  28DB'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  28DE'   22 2E47'              	SHLD	SBACK
  28E1'   3A 2E3E'              	LDA	SRNGF
  28E4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  28E5'   CA 28F7'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  28E8'   CD 21B7'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  28EB'   DA 2973'              	JC	SUBBAD
  28EE'   2A 2E3F'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  28F1'   CD 11E8'              	CALL	GOLN
  28F4'   C3 28FD'              	JMP	SUBLP
  28F7'                         SUB_1L:
  28F7'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  28FA'   22 2E49'              	SHLD	SLINES
  28FD'                         SUBLP:
  28FD'   CD 2979'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2900'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-127


  2901'   CA 290F'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2904'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2906'   32 2E45'              	STA	SANY
  2909'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  290C'   22 2E47'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  290F'                         SUB_NX:
  290F'   2A 2E49'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2912'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2913'   22 2E49'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2916'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2917'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2918'   CA 2936'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  291B'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  291E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  291F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  2922'   3A 2E45'              	LDA	SANY
  2925'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2926'   CA 2930'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  2929'   2A 2E47'              	LHLD	SBACK
  292C'   23                    	INX	H
  292D'   22 2E47'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  2930'                         SUB_AE:
  2930'   CD 0A84'              	CALL	ATEOF
  2933'   D2 28FD'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  2936'                         SUBDON:
  2936'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  2939'   3A 2E45'              	LDA	SANY
  293C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  293D'   CA 295B'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  2940'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2942'   32 2D7E'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  2945'   2A 2E47'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2948'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2949'   B5                    	ORA	L
  294A'   CA 2955'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  294D'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  294F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  2952'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  2955'                         SUB_PL:
  2955'   CD 1292'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  2958'   C3 07DD'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  295B'                         SUBNFD:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-128


  295B'   21 2E4F'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  295E'   CD 03D0'              	CALL	BACKTO
  2961'   2A 2E52'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  2964'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  2967'   21 26C1'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  296A'   CD 2637'              	CALL	EXERR
  296D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2970'   C3 2632'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  2973'                         SUBBAD:
  2973'   21 26AF'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  2976'   C3 262C'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  2979'                         SUBLIN:
  2979'   AF                    	XRA	A
  297A'   32 2E46'              	STA	SLCHG
  297D'   CD 29F8'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2980'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2983'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2983'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  2986'   DA 298E'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  2989'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  298B'   C2 2983'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  298E'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  298E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2991'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2992'   CD 183C'              	CALL	CEND
  2995'   D1                    	POP	D
  2996'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  2999'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  299A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  299D'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  299E'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  299F'   FA 29F1'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  29A2'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  29A3'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  29A4'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  29A7'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  29AA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  29AD'   DA 29F1'              	JC	SUBL_D
  29B0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  29B3'   EB                    	XCHG
  29B4'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  29B7'   EB                    	XCHG
  29B8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  29BB'   EB                    	XCHG
  29BC'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-129


  29BD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  29C0'   D1                    	POP	D
  29C1'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  29C2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  29C5'   3A 2E45'              	LDA	SANY
  29C8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29C9'   C2 29D6'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  29CC'   3A 2E46'              	LDA	SLCHG
  29CF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29D0'   C2 29D6'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  29D3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  29D6'                         SUBL_C:
  29D6'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  29D9'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  29DA'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  29DC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  29DF'   11 2E54'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  29E2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  29E5'                         SUBL_I:
  29E5'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  29E7'   32 2E46'              	STA	SLCHG
  29EA'   3A 2E44'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  29ED'   B7                    	ORA	A
  29EE'   C2 298E'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  29F1'                         SUBL_D:
  29F1'   CD 29F8'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  29F4'   3A 2E46'              	LDA	SLCHG
  29F7'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  29F8'                         SUBBOL:
  29F8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  29FB'   CD 0624'              	CALL	BOL
  29FE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2A01'                         VC_SRCH:
  2A01'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2A04'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2A05'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2A08'   22 2E34'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A0B'   C1                    	POP	B
  2A0C'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2A0D'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2A0F'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2A11'   CA 2A16'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2A14'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2A16'                         VS_DIR:
  2A16'   32 2E09'              	STA	SDIR
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-130


  2A19'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2A1C'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  2A1F'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2A20'   CD 1F60'              	CALL	RDLINE
  2A23'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A24'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2A25'   3A 2DE0'              	LDA	RLLEN
  2A28'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A29'   CA 2A3E'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2A2C'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2A2D'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  2A2F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2A32'   19                    	DAD	D
  2A33'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  2A35'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  2A36'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  2A39'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A3B'   32 2E0B'              	STA	SHAVE
  2A3E'                         VS_OLD:
  2A3E'   3A 2E0B'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2A41'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A42'   CA 2A4E'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  2A45'   3A 2E09'              	LDA	SDIR
  2A48'   32 2E0A'              	STA	SDIRE
  2A4B'   C3 2A77'              	JMP	SRCHN
  2A4E'                         VS_NOP:
  2A4E'   21 26F1'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2A51'   C3 2637'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  2A54'                         VC_N:
  2A54'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2A57'   3A 2E09'              	LDA	SDIR
  2A5A'   C3 2A64'              	JMP	VN_GO
  2A5D'                         VCB_N:
  2A5D'   CD 07A0'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2A60'   3A 2E09'              	LDA	SDIR
  2A63'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  2A64'                         VN_GO:
  2A64'   32 2E0A'              	STA	SDIRE
  2A67'   CD 06CB'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2A6A'   22 2E34'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A6D'   3A 2E0B'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2A70'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2A71'   CA 2A4E'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  2A74'   C3 2A77'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  2A77'                         SRCHN:
  2A77'   CD 127A'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2A7A'   CD 06F4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and an ESC may stop it
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-131


  2A7D'                         SN_LP:
  2A7D'   CD 2AB2'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2A80'   DA 2A9D'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2A83'   2A 2E34'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2A86'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2A87'   22 2E34'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2A8A'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2A8B'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2A8C'   C2 2A7D'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2A8F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2A92'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2A95'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2A97'   32 2E16'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2A9A'   C3 1292'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2A9D'                         SN_BAD:
  2A9D'   CD 0731'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2AA0'   DA 0749'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2AA3'   CD 073C'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2AA6'   21 26C1'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2AA9'   CD 2637'              	CALL	EXERR
  2AAC'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2AAF'   C3 2632'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2AB2'                         SRCHGO:
  2AB2'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2AB4'   32 2E3D'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  2AB7'   CD 2ACD'              	CALL	SRCHW
  2ABA'   D0                    	RNC
  2ABB'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2ABE'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2ABF'   3A 2E0A'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2AC2'   2F                    	CMA
  2AC3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2AC6'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2AC7'   32 2E3D'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2ACA'   C3 2ACD'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2ACD'                         SRCHW:
  2ACD'   3A 2E0A'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2AD0'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2AD3'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2AD6'   22 2D7B'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-132


  2AD9'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2ADA'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2ADD'                         SW_LP:
  2ADD'   CD 0735'              	CALL	BRKQ		; an ESC spent the count, here or on the way to
  2AE0'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2AE1'   CD 2AED'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2AE4'   D0                    	RNC
  2AE5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2AE8'   D2 2ADD'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2AEB'   37                    	STC
  2AEC'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2AED'                         SRCH1:
  2AED'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2AF0'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2AF3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2AF4'   FA 2B18'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2AF7'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2AFA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2AFD'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2B00'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2B01'   3A 2E3D'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2B04'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B05'   CA 2B0E'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2B08'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2B09'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2B0A'   37                    	STC
  2B0B'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2B0C'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2B0D'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2B0E'                         SR1_FW:
  2B0E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2B11'   D8                    	RC
  2B12'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2B15'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2B18'                         SR1_BK:
  2B18'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2B1B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2B1E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  2B21'   D8                    	RC
  2B22'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2B25'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2B28'   D8                    	RC
  2B29'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-133


                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2B2C'                         MSGNAM:
  2B2C'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B2D'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2B2F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  2B32'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B33'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  2B34'   23                    	INX	H
  2B35'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B36'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B38'   C2 2B45'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  2B3B'   21 275F'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  2B3E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2B41'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B42'   C3 2B8F'              	JMP	MN_END
  2B45'                         MN_HAS:
  2B45'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  2B47'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  2B4A'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B4B'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B4C'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  2B4D'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2B4E'   CA 2B5C'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  2B51'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B52'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  2B54'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B57'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  2B59'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B5C'                         MN_NAM:
  2B5C'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B5D'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2B5E'   23                    	INX	H
  2B5F'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  2B61'                         MN_NM:
  2B61'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B62'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B64'   CA 2B6F'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2B67'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B6A'   23                    	INX	H
  2B6B'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2B6C'   C2 2B61'              	JNZ	MN_NM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-134


  2B6F'                         MN_EXT:
  2B6F'   E1                    	POP	H
  2B70'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  2B73'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  2B74'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B75'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B77'   CA 2B8F'              	JZ	MN_END
  2B7A'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2B7C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B7F'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2B81'                         MN_XL:
  2B81'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2B82'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2B84'   CA 2B8F'              	JZ	MN_END
  2B87'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2B8A'   23                    	INX	H
  2B8B'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2B8C'   C2 2B81'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2B8F'                         MN_END:
  2B8F'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2B91'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2B94'                         MSGFLG:
  2B94'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2B96'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B97'   CA 2BA7'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2B9A'   3A 2D7E'              	LDA	MODF
  2B9D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2B9E'   CA 2BA7'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2BA1'   21 2769'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2BA4'   CD 2BBC'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2BA7'                         MF_RO:
  2BA7'   3A 2DF6'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2BAA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BAB'   CA 2BB4'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2BAE'   21 2774'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2BB1'   CD 2BBC'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2BB4'                         MF_NEW:
  2BB4'   3A 2DF4'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2BB7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BB8'   C8                    	RZ
  2BB9'   21 277F'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2BBC'                         MF_PUT:
  2BBC'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2BBD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2BBE'   C2 2BCA'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2BC1'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2BC3'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2BC4'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2BC6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BC9'   E1                    	POP	H
  2BCA'                         MF_STR:
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-135


  2BCA'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2BCD'                         EMITW:
  2BCD'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2BCE'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2BCF'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2BD1'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2BD4'   CD 2BF2'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2BD7'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2BDA'   CD 2BF2'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2BDD'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2BE0'   CD 2BF2'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2BE3'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2BE6'   CD 2BF2'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2BE9'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2BEA'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2BEC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2BEF'   D1                    	POP	D
  2BF0'   C1                    	POP	B
  2BF1'   C9                    	RET
  2BF2'                         EWDIG:
  2BF2'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2BF4'                         EW_LP:
  2BF4'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2BF5'   93                    	SUB	E
  2BF6'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2BF7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2BF8'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2BF9'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2BFA'   DA 2C01'              	JC	EW_DN
  2BFD'   0C                    	INR	C
  2BFE'   C3 2BF4'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2C01'                         EW_DN:
  2C01'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2C02'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2C03'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C04'   C2 2C0A'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2C07'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C08'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C09'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2C0A'                         EW_PR:
  2C0A'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2C0B'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2C0D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C10'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2C12'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2C13'                         ISEMPT:
  2C13'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2C16'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2C17'   B5                    	ORA	L
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-136


  2C18'   C0                    	RNZ
  2C19'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2C1C'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2C1D'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2C1E'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  2C1F'                         MSGCTG:
  2C1F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2C22'   CD 2B2C'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C25'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2C27'   CD 2B94'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2C2A'   CD 2C13'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2C2D'   C2 2C36'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  2C30'   21 2792'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  2C33'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  2C36'                         MG_POS:
  2C36'   21 2785'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  2C39'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2C3C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  2C3F'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  2C40'   CD 2BCD'              	CALL	EMITW
  2C43'   21 278C'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  2C46'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  2C49'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  2C4C'   23                    	INX	H
  2C4D'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2C4E'   CD 2BCD'              	CALL	EMITW
  2C51'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  2C54'   23                    	INX	H
  2C55'   D1                    	POP	D
  2C56'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  2C59'   C8                    	RZ
  2C5A'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2C5B'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  2C5D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2C60'   E1                    	POP	H
  2C61'   C3 2BCD'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  2C64'                         FILMSG:
  2C64'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2C67'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2C69'   C8                    	RZ
  2C6A'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2C6D'   CD 2B2C'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C70'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  2C71'   C3 2B94'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-137


                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  2C74'                         WRMSG:
  2C74'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2C77'   CD 2B2C'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2C7A'   21 27A4'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2C7D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2C80'                         MSGPST:
  2C80'   3A 2DF3'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2C83'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2C84'   C8                    	RZ
  2C85'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2C86'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2C87'   32 2DF3'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2C8A'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2C8B'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2C8D'   CA 2CA9'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  2C90'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  2C92'   CA 2CAF'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2C95'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2C97'   CA 2CA0'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2C9A'   CD 2C1F'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2C9D'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2CA0'                         MP_BIG:
  2CA0'   21 2740'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  2CA3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2CA6'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2CA9'                         MP_FIL:
  2CA9'   CD 2C64'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2CAC'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2CAF'                         MP_WRT:
  2CAF'   CD 2C74'              	CALL	WRMSG
  2CB2'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2CB5'                         VC_CTLG:
  2CB5'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2CB7'   32 2DF3'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2CBA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2CBC'   32 2D7F'              	STA	RINTENT
  2CBF'   C3 06CB'              	JMP	GETCNT		; a count typed before it is spent here, not
                                				;   left for the next command
                                
  2CC2'                         CMDTAB:
  2CC2'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  2CC3'   2CB5'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  2CC5'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2CC6'   081A'                 	DW	VC_H
  2CC8'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2CC9'   0844'                 	DW	VC_L
  2CCB'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2CCC'   0915'                 	DW	VC_J
  2CCE'   6B                    	DB	'k'
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-138


  2CCF'   0927'                 	DW	VC_K
  2CD1'   30                    	DB	'0'
  2CD2'   0877'                 	DW	VC_0
  2CD4'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  2CD5'   0895'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2CD7'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2CD8'   08D3'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2CDA'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2CDB'   08F7'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2CDD'   77                    	DB	'w'
  2CDE'   0B06'                 	DW	VC_W
  2CE0'   62                    	DB	'b'
  2CE1'   0B57'                 	DW	VC_B
  2CE3'   65                    	DB	'e'
  2CE4'   0CED'                 	DW	VC_E
  2CE6'   57                    	DB	'W'
  2CE7'   0B0D'                 	DW	VCBGW
  2CE9'   42                    	DB	'B'
  2CEA'   0B5E'                 	DW	VCBGB
  2CEC'   45                    	DB	'E'
  2CED'   0CF4'                 	DW	VCBGE
  2CEF'   47                    	DB	'G'
  2CF0'   11B8'                 	DW	VC_G
  2CF2'   67                    	DB	'g'
  2CF3'   120B'                 	DW	VC_LG
  2CF5'   78                    	DB	'x'
  2CF6'   17EF'                 	DW	VC_X
  2CF8'   5A                    	DB	'Z'
  2CF9'   1FD3'                 	DW	VC_BZ
  2CFB'   66                    	DB	'f'
  2CFC'   021A'                 	DW	VC_FF
  2CFE'   46                    	DB	'F'
  2CFF'   021F'                 	DW	VC_FB
  2D01'   74                    	DB	't'
  2D02'   0224'                 	DW	VC_TF
  2D04'   54                    	DB	'T'
  2D05'   0229'                 	DW	VC_TB
  2D07'   3B                    	DB	';'
  2D08'   0240'                 	DW	VC_SEMI
  2D0A'   2C                    	DB	','
  2D0B'   024A'                 	DW	VC_COMMA
  2D0D'   79                    	DB	'y'
  2D0E'   1AE1'                 	DW	VC_Y
  2D10'   59                    	DB	'Y'
  2D11'   1AE9'                 	DW	VY_YY
  2D13'   70                    	DB	'p'
